   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of Member States to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Association of Hotel, Restaurants and Caf&#xE9;s (HOTAM), a member of the Employers&#x2019; Organization in North Macedonia, implemented a pilot initiative to formalize workers, especially young people, in the Hotel, Restaurant and Catering (HORECA) sector in Ohrid and Struga. As a result, 136 individuals (68 female and 68 male) and 17 businesses operating a total of 26 hospitality establishments participated in the Pilot. Of 136 participants, 105 undeclared workers were registered as seasonal workers with fix-term contracts working full time, 40 hours per week; 20 participants declared as part time were registered as full-time workers, 40 hours per week; eight unregistered participants were registered as seasonal workers with fixed term contracts working 20 hours per week; two registered workers received an increase of 10 hours per week (previously undeclared), and in case of one participant the employer reported a higher salary than the minimum wage previously declared.  The experience gained from this pilot aiming to identify incentives and disincentives for short-term, seasonal and part-time workers and their employers has been discussed with the tripartite constituents and is been translated into a policy brief with evidence-based recommendations.
 
The Labour Inspectorate has increased capabilities in addressing undeclared work. Notably, 100 labour inspectors, (52 women) have undergone specialized training on employment relations and undeclared work, non-standard forms of employment, and working conditions; 13 training modules adapted from the ILO modules for labour inspectors have been developed; and an online learning platform has been made available to labour inspectors.  Moreover, the Labour Inspectorate has managed to increase its transparency and visibility by creating a new web platform consisting of a content management system-based website, and an administrative module to increase operational effectiveness. The ILO supported the implementation of a pilot activity aiming to identify incentives and disincentives for short-term, seasonal and part-time workers and their employers to formalize this work. The ILO conducted an assessment of this pilot and produced a policy brief with conclusions and recommendations.

The ILO, with the support of the European Union, has organized specialized training on employment relations and undeclared work, non-standard forms of employment, and working conditions, adapted the modules and has made available the online learning platform. The ILO has also created the new web platform.</narrative>
   </description>
