  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Increased capacity of Member States to improve social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Economic and Social Council  (ESC) adopted the ESC Action Plan on 13 March 2023 and increased the number of meetings from four (4) in 2022 to eight (8) in 2023 (until August 2023). ESC plays a more prominent role in shaping the economic and social policies, including the minimum wage, anti-crises relief packages, Youth Guarantee etc. ESC also led the consultative process for drafting the Labour Relations Law . ESC discussed and proposed ratification of three ILO conventions, with ILO C190 ratified in June 2023.

The country continued the fully inclusive and consultative process of drafting the new Labour Relations Law, aiming to fully align it with the ILS and the EU acquis. The current draft version has incorporated number of ILO recommendations (expected to enter Government procedure in September 2023).  

First General Collective Agreement for the Public Sector after 15 years was signed on 18 July 2023. 

Macedonian constituents used the new ILO tool for self-assessment of the labour disputes resolution mechanisms. They identified the strengths and weaknesses of the ASLD mechanism and based on the findings of the assessment subsequently developed Action Plan for reform and advancement of amicable settlement of labour disputes for the period 2023-2025.
 The ILO supported the process of resuming the regular meetings of the Economic Social Council (ESC), including tripartite retreat and development of ESC Action Plan and ESC Annual Work Programme and provided advice on topics discussed at the ESC meetings. The ILO provided technical comments on the draft Labour Relations Law and participated in the work of the tripartite working group and supported 11 public debates on the draft Labour Relations Law and one international conference.
The ILO supported development of website with all the labour legislation, International Labour Standards, European Union acquis and over 1000 relevant cases of court verdicts (including those based on ILO conventions).
The ILO supported self-assessment of the system of Amicable Settlement of Labour Disputes (ASLD), the development of the Action Plan on ASLD and supported peer learning and sub-regional exchange through the sub-regional meeting of the Western Balkan networks of Economic and Social Council&#x2019;s and ASLD institutions.
ILO staff  participated as resource person in two workshops organised by the TUs, one on General collective agreement  in public sector and one on General collective agreement  in private sector.


The ILO staff  participated as resource person in two workshops organised by the TUs, one on General collective agreement  in public sector and one on General collective agreement  in private sector.</narrative>
   </description>
